# J7 / L1.1 — what "the sweep succeeded" means here.
#
# HTTP 200 is a statement about the transport, not about the round. Most of these sweeps
# catch per row so one bad item cannot take the batch down, which means a round in which
# EVERYTHING failed answers 200 with a well-formed body — and sweep.sh used to write the
# heartbeat the container healthcheck reads on the strength of that 200. A dead flow and a
# quiet one were the same two files on disk.
#
# So every sweep below now answers with `ok`, decided in the service where the counters
# have names: false only when the round failed at something and accomplished nothing.
# Partial failure stays true on purpose — a scheduler pinned to unhealthy by one bad row
# reports an outage no more usefully than one that is always green.
#
# All eleven were checked (L1.1). Three never had a failure count at all and now do:
# subscriptions/process-due, orders/reminders/reorder, and daily-sales-broadcast — whose
# `skipped` conflated "this depot has no number" with "this depot's update threw", so on
# the live stack it answers {"attempted":0,"skipped":60} and that said nothing about which.
# Five already counted their failures honestly and nobody read them: outbox, webhooks,
# scheduled-reports, campaigns, fraud-flags (as `unavailable`). Two need no verdict —
# orders/internal/expire-abandoned and announcements/publish-due have no per-row catch, so
# a failure propagates and the exit code already carries it; leave them that way.
#
# Hydromart internal sweeps (busybox crond). Times are the container's TZ, which follows
# PRICING_TZ (Asia/Jakarta by default) — so every time below is a WIB business hour, the
# same clock the reports and day boundaries use. Logs go to the container.
#
# Place orders for all due recurring subscriptions — hourly.
0 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh subscriptions/process-due
# "Time to refill" reorder reminders — daily at 08:00.
0 8 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh orders/reminders/reorder
# Order side effects still owed (H-10): the stock consume, the loyalty award, the referral
# qualification, the franchise-owner credit. The happy path delivers these inline at
# completion, so this is purely the RETRY path — and until now it had no schedule at all,
# which meant a PENDING row with money owed against it sat there forever. Every 10 minutes:
# the sweep is bounded and idempotent (it claims rows), and an effect a customer is waiting
# on should not be a day late. `outbox/pending` reports the backlog if it ever stops draining.
*/10 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh orders/outbox/internal/process
# K2.2: fail non-cash payments left PENDING past their window. There was no sweep and no
# line here, so an abandoned transfer or QRIS attempt stayed PENDING for as long as the
# database did — and because `initiate` refuses to start a payment while an active one
# exists, that one dead row locked its order out of EVERY other payment method, forever,
# with nothing anywhere saying why. Hourly: the window is measured in hours, the tick is
# bounded at 500 rows, and whatever it does not reach is still stale next hour. CASH is
# never touched — a COD payment is PENDING by design until the courier confirms.
25 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh payments/internal/expire-pending payment:3005
# Release the stock held by orders nobody ever confirmed. Also unscheduled until now, so a
# reservation from an abandoned checkout was never given back — a slow leak into false
# out-of-stock. Hourly is enough: the threshold is measured in minutes-to-hours, and the
# sweep re-reads whatever is stale each time rather than tracking a cursor.
5 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh orders/internal/expire-abandoned
# J8: call someone about a delivery that is late RIGHT NOW. The SLA has always been a
# percentage in a report — computed the next morning, about deliveries long since over.
# Every 10 minutes because the point is the window while the order is still rescuable;
# hourly would mean an average half-hour of silence on top of an already-blown SLA. The
# sweep alerts each delivery once (it stamps `slaAlertedAt`), so the cadence costs
# nothing in repeat messages. It reports; it does not reassign anyone's work.
*/10 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh deliveries/internal/sla-sweep delivery:3006
# K4.2: apply agen price/status changes that were scheduled for a date that has arrived.
# Hourly, and on the hour: the console only lets staff pick a DATE, so anything finer
# would be precision the feature does not have. A change scheduled for today lands within
# the hour, the agen is told, and nobody has to be at a desk for it.
0 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh customers/internal/resellers/apply-scheduled customer:3002
# Retention enforcement (M23-21): runs the policy for every dataset that has an executor
# — including proof-of-delivery, which used to sweep itself at 03:15 off a separate
# per-depot window. One owner for the rule now. Datasets with no executor are logged as
# such. Daily at 03:30, after the 03:00 database backup, so a bad delete is always
# recoverable from that night's dump.
30 3 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh retention/internal/purge admin:3017
# Release HR announcements whose scheduledAt has come due (C1). Every 15 minutes: the
# console lets HR pick a time to the minute, and an hourly sweep would make "kirim jam 9"
# arrive at 10. Nothing is sent twice — publishedAt is the latch.
*/15 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh announcements/publish-due hr:3018
# Broadcast campaigns (B-17): send the next batch for every campaign still SENDING. The
# send endpoint only CLAIMS the campaign now — this sweep is what actually messages
# customers. Every 2 minutes: staff press "kirim" and expect it to be moving, not to wait
# an hour. Each tick is bounded and resumable, so an interrupted sweep loses nothing.
*/2 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh campaigns/internal/process-sending crm:3012
# Partner webhook fan-out (H-30): send every delivery whose next attempt is due. Every
# 5 minutes rather than hourly — a partner integration that learns about a delivery an
# hour late is not an integration. Retries back off on their own (1m, 5m, 25m, ...), so
# a dead endpoint is not hammered by this cadence.
*/5 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh webhooks/deliveries/process admin:3017
# Depot SOP: the twice-daily "laporan penjualan" — how many gallons each depot has sold so
# far today, sent to the depot's own number. 13:00 (siang) and 21:00 (sore) WIB. The slot
# is a path segment rather than a body because sweep.sh posts an empty one; it also gives
# the two runs separate locks, so a slow siang never swallows sore.
0 13 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh reports/internal/daily-sales-broadcast/siang
0 21 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh reports/internal/daily-sales-broadcast/sore
# Scheduled reports (15c): produce every report whose time has come. Hourly rather than
# at midnight because a cadence becomes due at a UTC boundary and one fixed run would mean
# a single missed tick costs a whole day; the sweep is idempotent — it stamps nextRunAt, so
# an extra tick finds nothing due. Offset off the hour so it does not pile onto :00.
17 * * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh scheduled-reports/internal/run-due admin:3017

# Fraud scan (15b): customers with repeated settled refunds over the configured window.
# Daily, not hourly — the window is 30 days, so an hourly sweep would re-read the same
# month 24 times to find the same people. It raises REVIEW items only; nothing it does
# blocks an account, and a case a human already has open is skipped rather than duplicated.
# 11:10 WIB, after the depots have opened and before HQ reviews the morning. The line used
# to read `10 4` with a comment calling it "04:10 UTC = 11:10 WIB" — but this file's times
# are the container's own clock, and the container is Asia/Jakarta (the deploy proves it
# every run). So it fired at 04:10 WIB, seven hours before it was meant to, with the depots
# still shut.
10 11 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh fraud-flags/internal/scan admin:3017

# PAR-05: birthday points (FR-091). Built, tested, idempotent per customer per year — and
# called by nothing, so no birthday point has ever been granted in production. Nobody
# noticed because there is no screen whose absence is felt. Daily at 07:00 WIB: a birthday
# is a whole day, and a greeting that arrives before the depots open reads as one.
0 7 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh profile/internal/birthday-rewards customer:3002
# PAR-01: loyalty point expiry (BR-014). Same story — implemented, idempotent, unreachable,
# so points have never expired and the liability has accrued since launch. Daily at 03:45,
# after the 03:30 retention purge so the two nightly writers never overlap.
#
# It ships INERT: the sweep runs and expires nothing until `pointExpirySweepEnabled` is set
# to 1. Connecting it live on the first deploy would expire every lot that has been quietly
# accumulating past its window, which is a decision about customers' balances and not a
# deployment detail. The response says `disabled: true` rather than "0 lots", so "switched
# off" and "nothing was due" are never the same line in the log.
45 3 * * * sh /scripts/sweep.sh loyalty/internal/expire loyalty:3010
